'''Burawi people of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands''' are an ethnic group in the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]], descended from settlers who came from the states of [[Southwestern Kaftia]] — primarily [[Burawa]] and [[Galwa]] — during and after the colonial period. They comprise an estimated 6.5 percent of the country's population, roughly 7,500 people as of 2025. Most families trace their origin to [[Galwa]], the western Burawi kingdom, rather than to the eastern Kingdom of Burawa. Galwans are Burawi; the distinction is political rather than ethnic, and the community preserves its Galwan origin in its family traditions.

The Burawi community descends from the colonial administrators, clergy, merchants, and settlers who staffed the [[Badweyn Islands]] colony and later the [[Capuyaquiran League]]. Although the colonial administration gave way to the islands' own governments, the community remained, forming the smallest of the islands' three principal ethnic groups. Burawi family names and Burawi Catholic religious practice remain central markers of the community's identity.

== Origins ==

The ancestors of the Burawi of the islands came from the Southwestern Kaftian peninsula, the region of [[Kaftia]] bounded by the [[Mesogean Sea]] to the south and the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] to the west and north. Most families trace their origin to [[Galwa]], the western Burawi kingdom created by the [[Burawi Imperial Split]] of 1709, with the remainder tracing to Burawa and other Southwestern Kaftian states. The Galwan majority reflects the kingdom's orientation toward western sea routes, which carried Galwan Burawi administrators and traders to the Capuyaquiran during the colonial and League periods. As Galwans are Burawi, the regional origin does not constitute a separate ethnicity.

== History ==

=== Colonial era ===

The [[Badweyn Islands]] colony, administered from the [[Lacashe]] province of [[New Burawa]], drew its colonial personnel — governors, military officers, plantation managers, and clergy — from the Burawi homeland. The principal island of the colony, [[Bolodun]], was separated administratively in 1695 and given its own governor, consolidating a colonial administrative class on the island.

=== League period ===

After the [[Capuyaquiran League]] was formed in 1755 under the Burawi protectorate, the islands' Burawi community remained in place, connected to the states of Southwestern Kaftia through trade and the [[Burawi Catholic Church]]. During the [[Kuvari War]] (1796–1801), the League provided military support to the Sudanese Kaftian administration on Bolodun, and Burawi colonial and church figures were engaged on the same side.

=== Modern era ===

After the islands joined the [[United Capuyaquiran Republic]] in 1832 and gained independence in 1926, the Burawi community remained a small but established minority. Burawi welfare and church institutions continued to bind the community to Southwestern Kaftia, and Burawi family and religious identity persisted through the twentieth century. The community's Galwan roots in particular have become a point of distinct identity, setting the islands' Burawi apart from their relatives in Burawa proper.

== Culture ==

The Burawi of the islands speak [[Burawi]] as their native language — the language which also serves as the national language of the islands. The community is predominantly [[Burawi Catholic Church|Burawi Catholic]]. Family traditions distinguish Galwan and Burawan origins — both Burawi — with most families tracing to Galwa rather than to the eastern kingdom, a distinction preserved in church registers and family memory.

